1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,557
Before bob, the idea it implements.

2
00:00:02,877 --> 00:00:06,734
An FPGA is hardware whose function is decided by memory.

3
00:00:07,054 --> 00:00:10,129
This chapter builds that idea from one logic gate.

4
00:00:11,700 --> 00:00:12,801
Take an AND gate.

5
00:00:13,121 --> 00:00:20,914
Its whole behaviour is a truth table: four rows, one for each combination of its two inputs, and one output bit per row.

6
00:00:21,367 --> 00:00:24,549
Store those four output bits in four memory cells.

7
00:00:24,869 --> 00:00:33,263
Then let the inputs choose which cell to read, with a tree of two-way multiplexers: b picks within each pair, a picks between the pairs.

8
00:00:33,583 --> 00:00:36,564
This circuit is called a lookup table, or LUT.

9
00:00:37,033 --> 00:00:42,795
Apply a equals one and b equals one, and the tree reads the last cell, which holds a one.

10
00:00:43,115 --> 00:00:51,093
Change the contents of the cells, and the same circuit becomes an OR gate, or an exclusive OR, or any other function of two inputs.

11
00:00:52,667 --> 00:00:59,647
A six-input LUT works the same way with sixty-four cells, so it can compute any function at all of six inputs.

12
00:00:59,967 --> 00:01:06,797
Its contents are written as one 64-bit number, called INIT, exactly as AMD's own tools write it.

13
00:01:07,267 --> 00:01:13,046
For an AND of inputs zero and one, bit i of INIT is one whenever the low two bits of i are both one.

14
00:01:13,366 --> 00:01:22,054
Every group of four bits reads one, zero, zero, zero, which is an eight in hexadecimal, so the whole table is sixteen eights.

15
00:01:23,633 --> 00:01:30,974
A table alone has no memory, so each LUT is paired with a flip-flop, which holds a value from one clock edge to the next.

16
00:01:31,294 --> 00:01:37,031
One configuration bit chooses whether the output is taken straight from the LUT, or from the flip-flop.

17
00:01:37,500 --> 00:01:49,508
Adding numbers through general lookup tables is slow, so FPGAs also give every LUT a dedicated carry path to its neighbour. bob copies AMD's design of that path; chapter three takes it apart.

18
00:01:51,067 --> 00:01:54,053
Lookup tables are useless until they are connected.

19
00:01:54,373 --> 00:02:00,331
Between them run wires, and wherever several wires could drive one input, there is a multiplexer.

20
00:02:00,651 --> 00:02:05,519
Its select lines are not driven by logic: they come from configuration bits.

21
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:08,416
Select one, and A drives C.

22
00:02:08,736 --> 00:02:11,233
Select two, and B drives C.

23
00:02:11,553 --> 00:02:22,374
Select zero picks a constant zero, so a fabric whose bits are all zero is quiet: nothing is connected to anything. bob relies on exactly that, as chapter four shows.

24
00:02:23,933 --> 00:02:33,886
Every choice in the fabric is a bit: every truth-table entry, every flip-flop option, every multiplexer select, every setting of a memory or multiplier.

25
00:02:34,206 --> 00:02:39,120
Together they are the configuration memory, and a bitstream is simply its contents.

26
00:02:39,600 --> 00:02:48,554
bob's configuration memory holds 68,096 bits, cut into 532 frames of four 32-bit words.

27
00:02:48,874 --> 00:02:53,682
Chapters eight and nine are about how those bits get in, and how we prove they arrived.

28
00:02:55,233 --> 00:03:11,929
So an FPGA is four things: logic blocks that compute, hard blocks for what logic does badly, routing to connect them, and the configuration memory that decides all of it. bob has all four, plus I/O pads and a clock, and the rest of this film takes them one at a time.

